{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network (2010) is a meticulously crafted drama that dissects the birth of facebook and the cutthroat world of silicon valley with razor-sharp precision. directed by david fincher, the film is a masterclass in storytelling, blending biting dialogue, slick cinematography, and a haunting score by trent reznor and atticus ross.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-best performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing the character’s brilliance, insecurity, and emotional detachment with unsettling accuracy. the film paints zuckerberg as a socially awkward prodigy whose ambition borders on ruthlessness—a man who revolutionizes human connection while alienating those closest to him. andrew garfield shines as eduardo severin, zuckerberg’s best friend and the moral compass of the narrative, whose tragic arc underscores the film’s themes of betrayal and loss.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally stellar, with armie hammer as twins cameron and tyler winklevoss (a role that demands physical and comedic finesse) and max minkowski as their cunning cohort divya narendra. even minor characters like john klarmann’s lawrence lessig and rooney mara’s lisa kahn are imbued with depth and purpose.\n\nfincher’s direction is sleek and deliberate, employing a muted color palette and dynamic framing to mirror the cold, calculated atmosphere of the tech world. the editing (by kirk baxter and angus wall) is a marvel, intercutting courtroom testimonies with the frenetic early days of facebook with seamless precision. meanwhile, reznor and ross’s score—a mix of industrial beats and melancholic synths—elevates the film to a near-operatic level of tension and pathos.\n\nthe script, penned by aaron sorkin, crackles with wit and venom, transforming legal proceedings into razor-edged verbal duels. lines like *\"you’re going to go through life thinking girls don’t like you because you’re a nerd. but it’ll be because you’re an asshole\"* and *\"i’m not being defensive! i’m being honest!\"* are delivered with such speed and precision that they become cultural touchstones.\n\non a thematic level, the social network is a scathing critique of ambition, masculinity, and the myth of the \"self-made\" man. it questions whether genius can coexist with empathy, and whether innovation is worth the collateral damage. the film doesn’t glorify its protagonist; instead, it leaves us to grapple with the cost of his brilliance.\n\nif there’s a flaw, it’s that the film occasionally veers into hyper-stylization—fincher’s signature darkness can feel a tad too deliberate at times, and some of the legal proceedings border on the convoluted. yet these are minor quibbles in a film that’s otherwise flawless in its execution.\n\nultimately, the social network is more than a biopic or a tech drama; it’s a razor-sharp exploration of human nature, ambition, and the dark underbelly of success. it’s a film that demands to be watched, analyzed, and rewatched—a modern classic that transcends its genre.\n\nrating: ★★★★★ (5/5)", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.37151509523391724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.004415880423039198}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -1.5305379629135132}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0010852882405743003}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-06}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -4.172316494077677e-06}, {"token": ")", "logprob": -0.5232279300689697}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.12163481116294861}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.018915625289082527}, {"token": " meticul", "logprob": -2.7548675537109375}, {"token": "ously", "logprob": -0.00013982271775603294}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0004988856380805373}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.6063730716705322}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.07034865021705627}, 